N2  - Introduction and Objective: Postoperative pain is one of the most prevalent and bothersome problems in surgery departments. Nowadays, there are various methods of acupuncture for relieving pain with low complications in respect to other routine postoperative analgesics. This would be useful especially for high risk patients for complications of analgesics such as transplantation recipe. Materials and Methods: The patients referred to our department with indication of inguinal surgery were included in the study randomly and divided in two groups, study and control. We use electroacupuncture for study group and sham acupuncture for control group. Postoperative pain was quantified by blind observer in both groups using VAS standard score, then compared. Results: Pain intensity and analgesic use was significantly higher in sham (control) group. Conclusion: Acupuncture in patients under inguinal surgery can reduce the need for analgesics for relieving pain postoperatively. UP-03.015 Is Trans-Obturator Tape (TOT) A Safe and Long-Term Effective Procedure? Talaulikar A, Bapat S, Sovani Y, Chaudhari H, Paramne V, Shah D, Deshmukh V Maharashtra Medical Research Society & Ratna Memorial Hospital, Pune, India Introduction and Objective: To assess the long-term functional outcomes and complications of TOT procedure. Materials and Methods: From March 2005 to Jan 2011, 73 female patients with stress urinary incontinence underwent a TOT procedure. All patients were operated by same surgeon. They were evaluated pre operatively and stress incontinence was confirmed by urodynamic study. There were 61 patients who had pure stress incontinence and 12 had mixed incontinence. All these 12 patients were treated with anticholinergics prior to surgery. Fourteen Fr Foley catheters was inserted at the beginning of the procedure. A standard outside in technique was done in all cases. The catheter was removed on post-operative day 1. All patients were reassessed at 1 month, 3 months and subsequently 6 monthly. The assessment was done by a clinical examination, a pad test, ultrasound for residual urine and uroflowmetry. Results: Mean operative time was 20 minutes. 61 patients were dry when followed up for nearly 6 years. Five did not show any improvement and had to undergo an alternative procedure (TVT). Seven patients had partial improvement. Three patients were wet after 2 years, 2 patients had tape erosion, one patient had postoperative retention and 4 patients had prolonged groin pain. Conclusions: Although the TOT is a safe procedure, a long-term follow-up is needed to assess its efficacy.
