N2  - Introduction and Aims: Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is a worldwide public health problem. Prevention of progression of renal disease (PDR) is still a major challenge in Nephrology. Hypertension is an important contributing factor in the pathogenesis of renal failure. The sympathetic nervous system (SNS) activity and nitric oxide (NO) play an important role in the pathogenesis of hypertension associated with CKD. Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) has been increasingly recognized as an effective therapeutic approach in several fields of medicine. Among its therapeutic strategies are eletroacunpucture (EA) and moxibustion (MO). The aim of this study was to investigate the renal and hemodynamic effects of EA and MO in an experimental model of CKD in rats. Methods: Male wistar rats were submitted to 5/6 nephrectomy (5/6 nx) for eight weeks and divided in three groups. Control (NX): only 5/6 nx; Sham- EA-MO (NX-AS): 5/6 nx and twice weekly 20min EA-MO session in shampoints; and EA-MO (NX-AM): 5/6 nx and twice weekly 20 min EA-MO session in three real acupuncture points. Serum creatinine, 24h-proteinuria, mean arterial blood pressure (MAP), renal sympathetic nerve activity (RSNA) and serum Nitric Oxide level measurement, glomerulosclerosis and tubulointerstitial fibrosis index were assessed. The statistical significance of the results was evaluated by ANOVA followed by Tukey test. Values were expressed as mean S.D. Results: (Table presented) Histopathology analysis showed that NX-AM group presented less glomerulosclerosis and tubulointerstitial fibrosis in comparison to the other groups. Conclusions: The acupuncture-treated group presented significant improvement in all measured parameters. Therefore, our results suggest that EA and MO had a benefic effect upon PDR in the experimental model of 5/6 nephrectomy by interference with RSNA and NO levels, both relevant participators in central regulation of hemodynamic functions.
