N2  - Pain has been characterized as the subjective, conscious expression of a stimulation, which causes a reaction of defense or escape. It is always accompanied by a disagreeable sentiment. Conventional medicine copes with pain initially by using pain-killers, however alternative methods of treatment have been developed. Acupuncture: maybe the most ancient method of self-treatment. There are many indications, which support the selection of acupuncture as the method of treatment while its interpretation is essentially based on the theories of local, regional and general reaction. Trans-cutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation (TENS): It is an appliance of electric, non-traumatical stimulation of the nerves of the human body by means of electrodes. It can be used under the supervision of a pathologist or a physiotherapist. Variations of the TENS, such as the Dorsal Column Stimulation, the Deep Brain Stimulation and the Implantations in Peripheral Nerves, have started to be developed, without however any impressive results. Placebo: It is a treatment designed to resemble the real one but it is known to be ineffective for the patient's situation its effectiveness is questionable, as is the Biofeedback. Hypnosis: It is about a situation of relaxation and selective concentration of attention with a simultaneously reduced conception of peripheral stimulations. It is reported as a method of coping with pain in the texts of Hippocrates. A variation is the less spread Guided Imagery.
