N2  - Fifty patients served as subjects in a pilot study for determining the effect of the hypothermic electrophoretic instrument over conventional pain treatment regimens. Before treatment, each subject was preexamined and given an adapted pain questionnaire to complete regarding his or her particular pain problem. The 50 subjects were randomly divided into a control and a treatment group. All 50 subjects had been previously treated by conventional medical procedures (i.e., medication, surgery, transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation, pain clinics, biofeedback, and psychiatry). All of these procedures failed to reduce the various pain syndromes of the subjects. In both the treatment and control groups, subgroup I received one hypothermic electrophoretic treatment each week for 10 weeks. Subgroup II received two hypothermic electrophoretic treatments each week for 5 weeks, and subgroup III received three hypothermic electrophoretic treatments each week for 2 weeks. The control groups received a placebo treatment. Additionally, treatments were at selected acupuncture points. All three treatment groups showed significantly lower pain levels. Posttest questionnaires indicated that all subjects in the treatment groups had benefited from the treatments, with the exception of one subject who dropped out of the study. Therefore, the results of this study indicate that hypothermic electrophoretic treatment may significantly reduce pain over conventional methods alone.
