N2  - The analgesic effect of the centrally acting analgesic tramadol hydrochloride was studied in 346 patients with dental pain of various etiologies who were undergoing routine dental work and in 12 healthy volunteers. The pain thresholds were measured by electrical stimulation using an EOM 3 pulp tester before, 30, 60, and 120 minutes after taking the study medication per os. Pain intensity was measured on each occasion using a five-point verbal rating scale as well as prior to commencement of the study. Additionally, an overall assessment of the trial medication as poor, satisfactory, or good was made by both the physician and patient at the end of the trial period. Twelve volunteers, aged 20 to 42 years, received a single oral dose of tramadol, which ranged from 0.7 to 4.2 mg/kg (100 to 300 mg in a single dose). Subsequently, 346 patients (aged 16 to 62 years) with dental pain resulting from caries or pulp/periodontal disease were given tramadol as a single dose, which did not exceed 2.5 mg/kg. Twelve patients with periodontal disease and nine patients with pain caused by complicated tooth or root extractions received, in addition to tramadol, a single oral dose of 400 mg of the nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug ibuprofen. Sixteen patients suffering from alveolitis were treated with transcutaneous electrical stimulation of the appropriate acupuncture points 20 minutes after the oral administration of tramadol in doses of 100 to 150 mg. In 15 patients, a 5% solution of tramadol was applied directly to the tooth pulp. The results in the volunteer group confirmed that tramadol should not be administered in single oral doses in excess of 2.5 mg/kg (recommended dose: 1-2 mg/kg). Topically applied tramadol did not produce effective analgesia. The analgesic effect of oral tramadol was not considered to be satisfactory in patients with deep caries and acute purulent pulpitis, however, good and satisfactory analgesia was obtained in patients suffering from chronic periodontitis, chronic pulpitis, and alveolitis. The duration of analgesia was found to depend on the intensity of the pain (3 to 4 hours for moderately severe pain). Analgesia was enhanced when transcutaneous electrical stimulation was used in addition to the administration of tramadol, and a lower dose of tramadol could be used. Analgesia was most effective when tramadol was combined with ibuprofen. We therefore conclude that tramadol may be a useful drug in the treatment of dental pain.
