N2  - The effects of electric acupuncture stimulation on the minimum alveolar anesthetic concentration (MAC) of halothane were studied in 7 healthy volunteers. The endotracheal tube with gas sampling tube was inserted and halothane concentration was changed in every 20 minutes to determine MAC. The endtidal halothane concentration was measured by the gas chromatograph. The electric stimulation of 30 V, 1 msec, 60 Hz was applied for 10 seconds to the left forearm of the subject to determine the body movement. MAC is determined by the mean of two adjacent concentration with which the subject did and did not respond to the stimulation. After the control of MAC of halothane was determined, the acupuncture needle were inserted into the bilateral Stomach Meridian 36. The acupuncture stimulation of 3Hz, 1.5 mA (mean) was delivered for 20 minutes by Tokki stimulator. After the measurement of post-acupuncture MAC, 0.4 mg naloxone was injected under acupuncture stimulation and the changes in MAC were investigated. As a result, the halothane MAC under acupuncture stimulation was reduced to 86.2±11.1% from the control value. After naloxone administration, the levels of MAC were elevated closely to the level of the control value in all cases and the elevation was 19.1±14.8%. Data indicated the acupuncture analgesia was established even when the subjects were unconscious and also naloxone antagonized the acupuncture analgesia. However, the magnitude of analgesic effects were small and much less than the effects under conscious state.
