T1  - An experimental study on electric acupuncture anesthesia (Japanese)
LA  - Japanese
KW  - acupuncture
KW  - normal human
KW  - theoretical study
N2  - The purpose of the present study is to determine the appropriate electric stimulation for acupuncture anesthesia in man. Chinese or Japanese acupuncture needles were inserted into 2 or 3 acupuncture points such as Gokoku, Kyokuchi in the hand or forearm in 4 healthy men and 3 women. Using these needles for electrodes, square wave pulses were discharged from a nihon koden's MSE 3 type electronic stimulator for 30 to 140 min. During the courses of the experiment, pulse wave form, voltage and frequency were continuously monitored by a Hitachi V 150 synchroscope. It may be concluded from the observation of 40 experiments that a frequency of 150 to 1,000 Hz, pulse width 0.1 to 0.15 msec, voltage 4 to 7 V and electrical current 2 to 5 mA are appropriate. Analgesia for surgery was not obtained but in some volunteers, skin suture procedures could be performed almost without pain.
